<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/williston-store.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/williston-store-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/williston-store.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="739" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><a href="/appraisal-representation/">Services</a><i aria-hidden="true">/</i><span aria-current="page">Commercial, HOA &amp; Multifamily Appraisal</span></nav><p class="kicker">Appraisal service</p><h1>Commercial, HOA &amp; Multifamily Claim Appraisal in North Dakota</h1><p class="lede">Larger buildings mean larger estimates and more places for scope to slip. We appraise disputed claims on commercial properties, associations and apartment buildings across North Dakota.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-line" href="/request-an-appraisal/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>For</dt><dd>Owners, boards &amp; managers</dd></div><div><dt>Buildings</dt><dd>Retail, office, multifamily</dd></div><div><dt>Focus</dt><dd>Scope, pricing &amp; code</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><h2>Commercial claims are different</h2>
<p>A disputed commercial or association claim can involve dozens of roof sections, several buildings, multiple trades and a policy with schedules, sublimits and coinsurance language that a homeowner never sees. A single mistaken assumption in the carrier’s estimate, repeated across every building, can move the number substantially. North Dakota Insurance Appraiser brings the same line-by-line discipline to these files that we bring to a single-family home, scaled to the property.</p>

<h2>Where commercial estimates fall short</h2>
<h3>Low-slope and membrane roofs</h3>
<p>EPDM, TPO, PVC and modified bitumen roofs are common on commercial buildings in Fargo, Bismarck, Grand Forks, Minot and smaller towns alike. Hail can bruise insulation boards beneath a membrane without leaving obvious surface damage. Core samples, moisture surveys and the condition of the cover board matter. So do details like tapered insulation, crickets, curbs, parapet flashing and edge metal that must be rebuilt to current code.</p>
<h3>Metal roofs and wall panels</h3>
<p>Standing-seam and through-fastened metal panels show hail as dents. Whether those dents are treated as cosmetic or functional often depends on policy language, and some commercial policies carry a cosmetic damage exclusion. We document dent density, panel profile and availability so the appraisal panel has what it needs to value whatever the policy covers.</p>
<h3>Multi-unit scope</h3>
<p>Apartment and townhome complexes frequently get estimated by sampling. If the sample understates the damage, the error is multiplied. We inspect and count rather than extrapolate where the damage pattern warrants it.</p>

<h2>Cost items that matter at scale</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>General conditions</strong> such as permits, dumpsters, lifts, cranes, temporary protection and site supervision</li>
<li><strong>Overhead and profit</strong> for a project that plainly needs a general contractor</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law costs</strong> when current code requires upgrades, subject to the coverage and limits in your policy</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal and logistics factors</strong> including winter conditions, short exterior work windows and travel to rural sites</li>
<li><strong>Tenant coordination</strong> and phasing for occupied buildings</li>
</ul>
<p>We also check whether the carrier’s estimate treats each building and roof section consistently. It is common to see one building scoped for full replacement and an identical neighbor, hit by the same storm, scoped for spot repairs with no explanation of why the damage differed.</p>

<h2>Working with boards and managers</h2>
<p>Associations often have a board, a property manager, a reserve study and residents who all want answers. We provide clear written findings that a board can review, explain the difference between our estimate and the carrier’s in plain terms, and keep the record organized for an appraisal panel. We do not advise on how an association should allocate costs among owners; that belongs to your governing documents and, where needed, your attorney.</p>

<h2>Our role and its limits</h2>
<p>As your appraiser, we value the loss. The panel’s award generally addresses the amount of loss, not coverage, exclusions, coinsurance penalties or claim handling. Business income and extra expense claims involve accounting questions that may call for a forensic accountant. If the dispute is really about coverage, a North Dakota attorney should be involved. We are not a law firm and not a public adjuster, and we do not guarantee any outcome.</p>

<div class="note"><b class="h">Worth knowing</b>Some commercial policies set different deductibles for wind and hail, sometimes as a percentage of the building value. Check the declarations early so the expected net payment is clear.</div>

<h2>Helpful documents</h2>
<ul>
<li>Full policy with schedules, endorsements and statements of values</li>
<li>Roof warranties, as-built drawings and maintenance records</li>
<li>Carrier estimates, engineer reports and correspondence</li>
<li>Any bids from roofing or restoration contractors</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can you appraise a claim covering several buildings?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. We inspect each building, estimate each separately and roll them into one organized submission so the panel can follow every line.</p></div></details><details><summary>Does appraisal address a cosmetic damage exclusion?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Whether an exclusion applies is a coverage question. The panel values the loss; disputes over exclusions belong with your carrier or a North Dakota attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do you work with property managers?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Yes. With the owner or board’s authorization, we coordinate access, document requests and updates through the property manager.</p></div></details></div>
